{"records":[{"id":"rhsw3nbjhz","title":"Essential gene profiles for human pluripotent stem cells identify uncharacterized genes and substrate dependencies. Mair et al.","description":"Human pluripotent stem cells (hPSCs) provide an invaluable tool for modeling diseases and hold promise for regenerative medicine. For understanding pluripotency and lineage differentiation mechanisms, a critical first step involves systematically cataloging genes that are indispensable for hPSC maintenance and proliferation. To map genetic determinants of hPSC fitness, we performed genome-scale loss-of-function screens in an inducible Cas9 H1 hPSC line cultured on feeder cells and feeder-free to identify essential genes. Among these, we found FOXH1 and VENTX, genes that encode transcription factors previously implicated in stem cell biology, as well as an uncharacterized gene, C22orf43/DRICH1. hPSCs essential genes are substantially different from other cell lines, and gene essentiality in hPSCs is highly context-dependent with respect to different growth substrates. Our CRISPR screens establish parameters for genome-wide screens in hPSCs, which will facilitate the characterization of unappreciated regulators of hPSC biology to understand the genetic wiring of hPSC fitness and pluripotency.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2019-01-24T11:57:50.932Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/rhsw3nbjhz","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/rhsw3nbjhz"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Barbara","last_name":"Mair","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Jelena","last_name":"Tomic","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Sanna","last_name":"Masud","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Jason","last_name":"Moffat","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Stem Cell","Functional Genomics","CRISPR"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"k6wm46g4tw","title":"Data set for the paper entitled \"CRISPR-enabled genetic screens identify synthetic lethal targets across frequently altered cancer drivers\" by Desjardins, Bowlan, et al. (2026)","description":"We undertook a series of genome-wide CRISPR screens using functionally validated isogenic cell lines and mapped SL interactions across 15 genetic alterations characteristic of diseases with high incidence and unmet clinical need: FBXW7, CCNE1, CDK12, ARID1A, KMT2D, DNMT3A, TET2, KEAP1, STK11, IDH1, SF3B1, SRSF2, U2AF1, chromosome 18q loss, and chromosome 13q loss. The data deposited here make the raw read count files available for all CRISPR screens conducted in the study. ","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-01-26T11:43:11.208Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/k6wm46g4tw","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/k6wm46g4tw"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Jordan","last_name":"Young","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Alejandro","last_name":"Álvarez-Quilón","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Jessica","last_name":"Desjardins","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Julian","last_name":"Bowlan","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Primary Data"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"pm5v6pwkkg","title":"Genome-wide screens implicate loss of cullin ring ligase 3 in persistent proliferation and genome instability in TP53 deficient cells","description":"TP53 deficiency is the most common alteration in cancer, however, alone is typically insufficient to drive tumorigenesis. To identify genes promoting tumorigenesis in combination with TP53 deficiency, we performed genome-wide CRISPR/Cas9 knockout screens coupled with proliferation and transformation assays in isogenic cell lines. Loss of several known tumor suppressor genes enhanced cellular proliferation and transformation. Surprisingly, loss neddylation pathway genes, promoted uncontrolled proliferation exclusively in TP53-deficient cells. CUL3 coupled with TP53 loss results in an oncogenic transcriptional program governed by the NFκB, AP-1 and TGF-β pathways. This program maintains persistent cellular proliferation, induces partial epithelial to mesenchymal transition, increases DNA damage, genomic instability, and the rate chromosomal rearrangements. Our findings reveal CUL3 loss as a key event stimulating persistent proliferation in TP53 deficient cells. These findings may be clinically relevant, since TP53-CUL3 deficient cells are highly sensitive to ATM inhibition, exposing a vulnerability that could be exploited for cancer treatment. ","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2020-11-06T17:31:10.704Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/pm5v6pwkkg","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/pm5v6pwkkg"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Alexandros","last_name":"Drainas","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Western Blot","Cellular Imaging"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"xn3vv2cdnk","title":"High-throughput CRISPR screens to dissect macrophage-Shigella interactions","description":"The positive hits, negative hits, and non-targeting controls of genome-wide CRISPR screens; Supplemental Tables.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2021-07-23T06:18:30.182Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/xn3vv2cdnk","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/xn3vv2cdnk"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Yong","last_name":"LAI","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Genetic Screening","Host-Pathogen Interaction","CRISPR"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"9x47wzy8x7","title":"A study of \"Genome-wide CRISPR screens reveal host factors critical for SARS-CoV-2 infection\" by Wei et al. Cell 2020","description":"Identification of host genes essential for SARS-CoV-2 infection may reveal novel therapeutic targets and inform our understanding of COVID-19 pathogenesis. Here, we performed genome-wide CRISPR screens in Vero-E6 cells with SARS-CoV-2, MERS-CoV, bat coronavirus HKU5 expressing the SARS-CoV-1 spike, and VSV expressing the SARS-CoV-2 spike. Together this identifies potential therapeutic targets for SARS-CoV-2 and reveals SARS-lineage specific and pan-coronavirus host factors that regulate susceptibility to highly pathogenic coronaviruses.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2020-10-15T21:29:14.06Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/9x47wzy8x7","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/9x47wzy8x7"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Craig","last_name":"Wilen","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["CRISPR","Middle East Respiratory Syndrome-Related Coronavirus","Severe Acute Respiratory Syndrome Coronavirus 2","COVID-19"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"hfbhym45fz","title":"CRISPR screening reveals a requirement for ribosome recycling in efficient SARS-CoV-2 programmed ribosomal frameshifting and viral replication. Rehfeld et al.","description":"Supplemental Table S1. Results of SARS-CoV-2 programmed ribosomal frameshifting genome-wide CRISPR screens (MAGeCK analysis).\nSupplemental Table S2. Oligonucleotides.\n","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2023-01-19T12:17:58.922Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/hfbhym45fz","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/hfbhym45fz"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Joshua","last_name":"Mendell","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Frederick","last_name":"Rehfeld","orcid":null}],"funders":[{"id":"https://ror.org/0003xa228","name":"Cancer Prevention and Research Institute of Texas","grant_id":"RP220309"},{"id":"https://ror.org/00np6vq88","name":"Welch Foundation","grant_id":"I-1961-20210327"},{"id":"https://ror.org/006w34k90","name":"Howard Hughes Medical Institute","grant_id":null}],"categories":["Oligonucleotide","CRISPR"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"8xhv88c7wt","title":"Genome-wide screening in human kidney organoids identifies developmental and disease-related aspects of nephrogenesis","description":"Human organoids allow the study of proliferation, lineage specification, and three-dimensional tissue development. Here, we present a genome-wide CRISPR screen in iPSC-derived kidney organoids. The combination of inducible genome editing, longitudinal sampling and endpoint sorting of tubular and stromal cells generated a complex, high-quality dataset uncovering a broad spectrum of insightful biology from early development to ‘adult’ epithelial morphogenesis. Our functional dataset allows improving mesoderm induction by ROCK inhibition, contains monogenetic and complex trait kidney disease genes, confirms two additional CAKUT genes (CCDC170 and MYH7B), and provides a large candidate list of ciliopathy-related genes. Finally, the identification of a cis-inhibitory effect of Jagged1 controlling epithelial proliferation shows how mosaic knockouts in pooled CRISPR screening can discover ways of communication between heterogeneous cell populations in complex tissues. Collectively, these data serve both as a rich resource for the kidney community and as a benchmark for future iPSC-derived organoid CRISPR screens.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2021-11-29T13:48:59.335Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/8xhv88c7wt","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/8xhv88c7wt"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Rosemarie","last_name":"Ungricht","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Philipp","last_name":"Hoppe","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Screening","CRISPR"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"zg488kvchw","title":"Untargeted Salivary Metabolomic Profiling Uncovers Metabolic Dysregulation in Oral Squamous Cell Carcinoma Patients","description":"Raw mass spectrometry data of saliva samples from human subjects across three groups - healthy controls, oral potentially malignant disorders (OPMD), and oral squamous cell carcinoma (OSCC). Untargeted metabolomic profiling was carried out by liquid chromatography-high-resolution mass spectrometry (LC-HRMS) on Agilent 6545 Q-TOF for the manuscript entitled \"Untargeted Salivary Metabolomic Profiling Uncovers Metabolic Dysregulation in Oral Squamous Cell Carcinoma Patients”. ","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-06-18T10:44:14.878Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/zg488kvchw","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/zg488kvchw"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Akanksha","last_name":"Bhattacharya","orcid":"0009-0003-1771-9732"},{"first_name":"Maynak","last_name":"Pal","orcid":"0000-0001-9467-517X"},{"first_name":"Ganakalyan","last_name":"Behera","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Deepti","last_name":"Joshi","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Anshul ","last_name":"Rai","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Ashok","last_name":"Kumar","orcid":"0000-0001-7208-2947"}],"funders":[],"categories":["Metabolomics","Oral Cancer","Oral Squamous Cell Carcinoma"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"3sdsc83vsn","title":"Genome-Scale Exon Perturbation Screens Uncover Exons Critical for Cell Fitness","description":"CRISPR-Cas technology has transformed functional genomics, yet understanding how individual exons differentially shape cellular phenotypes remains limited. Here, we optimized and conducted massively parallel exon deletion and splice site mutation screens in human cell lines to identify exons that regulate cellular fitness. Fitness-promoting exons are prevalent in essential and highly expressed genes, and commonly overlap with protein domains and interaction interfaces. Conversely, fitness-suppressing exons are enriched in non-essential genes, exhibiting lower inclusion levels, and overlap with intrinsically disordered regions and disease-associated mutations. In-depth mechanistic investigation of the TAF5 alternative exon-8, screen hit, revealed that its inclusion is required for assembly of the TFIID general transcription initiation complex, thereby regulating global gene expression output. Collectively, our orthogonal exon perturbation screens established a comprehensive repository of phenotypically important exons and uncovered regulatory mechanisms governing cellular fitness and gene expression.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2024-06-20T08:55:47.576Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/3sdsc83vsn","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/3sdsc83vsn"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Mei-sheng","last_name":"Xiao","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Arun Prasath","last_name":"Damodaran","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Bandana","last_name":"Kumari","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Ethan","last_name":"Dickson","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Kun","last_name":"Xing","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Tyler","last_name":"On","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Nikhil","last_name":"Parab","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Helen E. ","last_name":"King","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Alexendar R.","last_name":"Perez","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Wilfried M. ","last_name":"Guiblet","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Gerard","last_name":"Duncan","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Anney","last_name":"Che","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Raj","last_name":"Chari","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Thorkell","last_name":"Andresson","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Joana A.","last_name":"Vidigal","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Robert J.","last_name":"Weatheritt","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Michael","last_name":"Aregger","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Thomas","last_name":"Gonatopoulos-Pournatzis","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Microscopy"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"dbjrf4kgw5","title":"Literature-search dataset for a narrative review of connexin and pannexin channels in head and neck / oral squamous cell carcinoma","description":"This dataset contains the complete literature-search materials underlying a mechanistic narrative review of connexin- and pannexin-family channels in head and neck and oral squamous cell carcinoma (HN-/OSCC) and their proposed link to calcium and Hippo/YAP signalling. Seven databases were searched from inception to October 2026: PubMed/MEDLINE, OpenAlex, Europe PMC, Scopus, Web of Science Core Collection, Cochrane CENTRAL and Embase. A primary strategy combined a channel-family concept block (connexin, Cx43/Cx26/Cx30/Cx32/Cx45/Cx46, GJA1/GJB2, gap junction, hemichannel, pannexin/PANX1) with a head-and-neck site block; supplementary searches combined the channel block with cancer-stem-cell, Hippo/YAP and epithelial–mesenchymal-transition/invasion blocks with the site restriction removed, to capture mechanistically relevant evidence from other tumour types. After de-duplication by DOI and normalised title, 5,711 unique records were screened on title and abstract, yielding 56 cited references; six further references were added during revision: two via a supplementary semantic-search check, three via a supplementary sweep for unspaced isoform spellings (two outside the systematic-search corpus, one within it but previously uncited), and one genome-wide CRISPR fitness-screen study in OSCC, for 62 cited in total.\n\nThe deposit provides, for full reproducibility: the exact query strings for every database and search strand; the raw per-database record exports (one RIS file per database × strand, with provenance preserved); the single de-duplicated record set; the final cited-reference library; the search protocol; and the review's figures with legends. It is a structured narrative search with scoping-style breadth, not a registered systematic review — no formal risk-of-bias appraisal or meta-analysis was performed, and other-tumour evidence is included only as explicitly flagged extrapolation. ","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-10-16T12:49:10.9Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/dbjrf4kgw5","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/dbjrf4kgw5"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Yee Fan","last_name":"Choon","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Surendra Kumar ","last_name":"Acharya","orcid":"0000-0003-3387-8920"},{"first_name":"Chee Weng","last_name":"Yong","orcid":"0000-0001-9905-1585"},{"first_name":"Fong Fong ","last_name":"Liew","orcid":"0000-0001-9165-2343"}],"funders":[],"categories":["Oncology","Signal Transduction","Literature Review","Oral Cancer","Head and Neck Cancer"],"journal_issn":[]}],"total_records":139909,"page":1,"page_size":10,"execution_time":169,"aggregations":{"data_type_stats":{"dataset":139909,"collection":0},"access_type_stats":{"open":139178,"restricted":68,"embargoed":663},"top_funders_stats":[{"id":"https://ror.org/01h0zpd94","name":"National Natural Science Foundation of China","count":2343},{"id":"https://ror.org/003x0zc53","name":"Agencia Estatal de Investigación","count":1336},{"id":"https://ror.org/01cwqze88","name":"National Institutes of Health","count":690},{"id":"https://ror.org/03ha2q922","name":"National Science Center","count":482},{"id":"https://ror.org/02ddkpn78","name":"Fundação de Amparo à Pesquisa do Estado de São Paulo","count":444},{"id":"https://ror.org/00x0ma614","name":"Coordenação de Aperfeicoamento de Pessoal de Nível Superior","count":427},{"id":"https://ror.org/03swz6y49","name":"National Council for Scientific and Technological Development","count":414},{"id":"https://ror.org/021nxhr62","name":"U.S. National Science Foundation","count":394},{"id":"https://ror.org/018mejw64","name":"Deutsche Forschungsgemeinschaft","count":334},{"id":"https://ror.org/00hhkn466","name":"Japan Society for the Promotion of Science","count":282}],"source_stats":[{"id":"MENDELEY_DATA","name":"Mendeley Data","count":137255},{"id":"f881433a-b1cd-40d2-ab9f-4fd501f2d14b","name":"Elsevier Data Repository","count":1910},{"id":"a191461c-0227-403f-a1e9-621e0e0429cc","name":"San Raffaele Open Research Data Repository","count":188},{"id":"b07018a5-fee6-4df1-9d43-ff0d81ad75ed","name":"Bicocca Open Archive Research Data","count":138},{"id":"df228bbb-ac66-410f-8c66-f61608d63d95","name":"University of South Florida","count":78},{"id":"2a9d1b7d-e447-466f-888f-aa6f84e575e4","name":"Digital Commons Data@Becker","count":66},{"id":"72c28c45-627f-446b-8a48-af7c0a51a357","name":"Università Cattolica del Sacro Cuore","count":58},{"id":"b121dfec-bb51-4f19-a2f6-bc9ce0197286","name":"Babeș-Bolyai University","count":41},{"id":"7e51a2dd-133e-43c6-a220-12c1958e202a","name":"University of Canberra","count":31},{"id":"5f1edf21-7948-4d82-894f-c0ed4b1e8f52","name":"Teesside University","count":25}],"top_institutions_stats":[{"id":"ecb64011-71f0-5223-b4e4-4d27d5f51d83","name":"Universidad Autonoma de Madrid Centro de Biologia Molecular Severo Ochoa","count":871},{"id":"9d63ad1d-c391-5c35-a12e-cafe17017098","name":"Universidad Autonoma de Madrid","count":796},{"id":"3bfef41a-d03e-5722-bd55-dd245235fa78","name":"Bina Nusantara University","count":530},{"id":"0ad97c43-5173-56e6-bbe6-46dad5323862","name":"Daffodil International University","count":408},{"id":"55ba66b2-f276-5204-a3d8-acfa499794e0","name":"The University of Manchester","count":364}]}}